is_anomaly: false
faulty_devices: none
diagnosis_text:
I verified end-to-end reachability and the routing control plane. Both directions are healthy: pc1 (195.11.14.2) can ping pc2 (200.1.1.2) and pc2 can ping pc1 with 0% loss in each direction. On the routers, BGP is up and symmetric: router1 (AS 1) has BGP-learned 200.1.1.0/24 via 193.10.11.2 on eth0, and router2 (AS 2) has BGP-learned 195.11.14.0/24 via 193.10.11.1 on eth0. “show ip bgp summary” on both sides confirms the single eBGP neighbor is Established with one prefix received and prefixes sent. Kernel routing tables reflect this, and host defaults are correct (pc1 via 195.11.14.1, pc2 via 200.1.1.1). 

If the reported issue were an asymmetric return-path failure, we would see pc2→pc1 traffic drop and no BGP route on router2 for 195.11.14.0/24, or a missing local route on router1 to 195.11.14.0/24. None of these are observed. Packets from pc2 take router2→router1→pc1 and the reverse path exists identically for pc1→pc2.

Root cause: No active fault; the network is operating normally. The prior symptom was likely transient or a client-side misobservation.

Corrective fix: No restart is required. Recommend no action. If the issue recurs, capture timestamps and packet traces on router2 eth0 and router1 eth0 to catch intermittent control-plane flaps or host firewall behavior at the moment of failure.
